## Notes from building it

First project in TypeScript, coming from Python and GameMaker. Most of
the interesting problems were in the detection side rather than the
hiding side.

**The detector saturated before it was useful.** The first scoring
function scaled linearly and clamped at 1.0, so clean and embedded
images both maxed out and looked identical. The detector was working
fine — the scale just had no room left to show a difference. Rewrote it
to measure how close an image sits to the 50/50 pattern that embedding
produces, rather than scaling the raw ratio upward.

**The threshold was guesswork.** I'd set the suspicious/not-suspicious
cutoff at 0.6 by feel. Working through the math afterwards showed that
fully-embedded data lands at about 0.607 — close enough to the cutoff
that the result was near-random. It's now documented as provisional,
because picking a real threshold means testing against a set of actual
photographs, not synthetic test images.

**One failing test was the test's fault, not the code's.** A capacity
check expected -9 and got 0. Tracing it through, 0 was right: text
under 79 characters can't hold even the header, so its usable capacity
is genuinely zero. Easy to reflexively "fix" working code until a test
goes green, which just gets you a broken function and a checkmark.

**TypeScript caught something I wouldn't have thought to check.** The
type system distinguishes between regular and shared memory buffers,
and the Web Crypto API refuses shared ones — a second thread could
alter your data mid-encryption. That's a real bug class, flagged at
compile time rather than discovered later.

**Testing and deployment.** 38 tests covering round trips, failure
cases (wrong password, tampered data, oversized payloads), and the
properties that make it steganography at all: pixels change by at most
1, transparency is never touched, and the visible text is byte-for-byte
unchanged after embedding. GitHub Actions runs the suite on every push
and blocks the deploy if anything fails.